# Unitier R4d-a + R4d-b against main 347a86e5: the residency lane's victim fill runs on the NVMe tier's reader as a
# Victim job ranked after prompt jobs (the base read it on the lane thread through FillBy::Caller), and the step port's
# ensure is ensure_pinned, its ids held until the leg's reads end. R4d-b removes only a serve seat's refusal, which
# generate_qwen3moe never ran. Host only (no kernel moved: ptx-scan pairs identical), on the step's dispatch path, so
# this is the same-lease A/B AGENTS.md asks for. The base is built from 347a86e5 with `just build-decode` in its own
# worktree and remote dir.
#
# Shape: r4b-decode.card's but n 1024 (A6000, plan a, ctx 3072, prose 512, n 1024, BLOOMERY_RESIDENCY=mid-p0-s1, BLOOMERY_DRAFT=off,
# one slot, BLOOMERY_HOST_ROOM=27G with BLOOMERY_NVTIER_BYTES=20G). [derived, code] Both builds plan the same bytes:
# R4d moves no placement line, and with BLOOMERY_NVTIER_BYTES set nvme_arena_of returns the given bytes.
# Why n 1024, not r4b's 96 (the tier engine design, scratchpad keep/tierengine-design.md §4): in the 96 tokens after a P 512
# prompt the prompt's keep set covers the decode's first touches, so decode fills are about 0 and the victim reader path
# and the pinned step port run beside no fill traffic. Past that window the arena (S about 141 a layer against about 212
# tier ids) misses and every fill evicts, so the step's pins and the lane's victims run beside the fills they guard
# against. ctx 3072 holds P 512 + n 1024.
#
# [derived, code] Per token, what R4d does to the decode chain:
#   1. The step port's pin. ensure_pinned reads its misses on the resident pool, as ensure does (nvtier.rs, the doc of
#      ensure_pinned), so the decode fills' path and count are the base's. Added per paged layer a step: the pin and
#      unpin of the call's ids under the books' lock, a Vec of the ids and the guard's box (two allocations).
#      2..10 us a layer [assumption: one uncontended lock hold and two small allocations], 48 layers: 0.1..0.5 ms of a
#      72..92 ms token, -0.7..-0.1 %. With fills the pin is taken by the same call that fills, so it adds no wait.
#   2. The victim fill on the reader. A victim is filled before the lane's window opens (the flip's order); the
#      lane runs beside the step, and a late flip is the only way it reaches the chain. r4da measured (real gate_nvtier,
#      untimed, the gate plan) late 0 over 552 flips, victim wait 2.41 ms a victim against the lane's 156..217 ms
#      issue-to-boundary window (excl-decode.card:31). The reader's 16 threads are idle in decode (no prompt job), so a
#      victim job takes no pool thread from the step. 0.
#   3. A claim of a victim can no longer take a slot the step pinned. In the base it could, and the step then read a
#      re-filled slot: a correctness window, not a rate term. 0.
#   ~~Net -0.7..+0.0 %, central -0.3.~~ Struck 2026-10-11: term 2 missed the base's read path. Base 347a86e5
#   nvtier.rs:1598-1621 (open_read) reads a promotion the arena has not filled through the model file's mapping
#   (mapping_reads), so its lane faults pages in beside the step's fills. R4d-a fills it on the reader first. Measured
#   in the void sitting below, per row, every round: majflt base 45k-133k against R4d-a 27-1.7k; drive_read_bytes base
#   82-349 GB against R4d-a 40-93 GB. The rate term this moves is not measured: no speed claim until a residency-off or
#   fixed-id-stream decode card reads it.
#
# Sitting 2026-10-11 (6 rounds, A6000): VOID, no verdict. [other-busy] 5/12, and the greedy text differed per row inside
#   each arm (distinct_tokens 142-403 and 103-414): under residency a late flip decides whether an expert runs on the
#   card or the host, so the bits follow the lane's timing and "both arms print the same greedy tokens" cannot hold.
#   Realized paired sd about 22 %. card.py's fail line is not a reading.
# Priced 0: the reader's wake of all 16 threads per job (notify_all), which a victim job now pays and the lane thread's
# own read did not; a decode token's victims are few (r4da: 553 jobs over 96 steps, about 5.8 a token), each a
# wake of idle threads off the chain.
#
# Ruler: sd 2.6 % (excl-decode.card's, the residency pair). A noninf card: the changed arm may lose at most the
# margin. At 6 rounds, paired, h_p = t(0.975, 5) x 2.6 x sqrt(2/6) = 3.86 % (card.py check); the band's lower edge
# -0.7 less h_p is -4.56 %, inside the 5 % margin.
#
# Runner (the lead; output to a file first):
#   BLOOMERY_BOX_ENV='BLOOMERY_LEASE_CARD=docs/cards/r4d-decode.card BLOOMERY_GEN_CTX=3072 BLOOMERY_DRAFT=off BLOOMERY_DECODE_N=1024 BLOOMERY_AB_LOAD=arm BLOOMERY_AB_WARMUP=1' \
#   BLOOMERY_AB_ROUNDS=6 just depth-gpu-qwen4exp \
#     bin:/root/repo/bloomery-r4d-base/target/release/generate_qwen3moe:prose:512@BLOOMERY_RESIDENCY=mid-p0-s1,BLOOMERY_HOST_ROOM=27G,BLOOMERY_NVTIER_BYTES=20G \
#     prose:512@BLOOMERY_RESIDENCY=mid-p0-s1,BLOOMERY_HOST_ROOM=27G,BLOOMERY_NVTIER_BYTES=20G
kind: noninf
question: on a paged plan with an arena and residency set, do the victim fill on the tier's reader and the pinned step
  port leave the plain decode no slower than the base by more than the margin?
term: decode tok/s @ n=1024, prose 512 (the corpus's first 512 ids), ctx 3072, A6000, plan a at the BLOOMERY_HOST_ROOM
  27G with BLOOMERY_NVTIER_BYTES=20G (a 20 GiB arena), BLOOMERY_RESIDENCY=mid-p0-s1, BLOOMERY_DRAFT=off, one slot; this
  tree's generate_qwen3moe (7cec21ad, R4d-a) against 347a86e5's, (ours - base) / base, paired by round
unit: %
predict: -0.7..0
rounds: 6
sd: 2.6 % rig-log 09-30#q38seed-ab, the residency-only P 512 pair, as excl-decode.card and r4b-decode.card
margin: 5 % <the derivation allows under 1 %; at the residency pair's scatter a tighter margin needs 13+ rounds a
  side, and the card exists to catch a lateness or contention term the derivation missed, which would be large>
condition: one lease; both builds with their Cargo.lock current; every arm a fresh process (BLOOMERY_AB_LOAD=arm),
  the order rotated each round, one warm-up round discarded; no [cpu-busy], [other-busy] or [cold] row and no Xid in
  either witness. Both arms' plan lines and nvtier budget= and paged= fields are equal, each arm's `residency lever`
  record names mid-p0-s1; both arms print the same greedy tokens (R4d moves no bit). Read beside the ratio, not
  judged: each arm's residency late count and nvtier fills
decide-in: land R4d with the row (decode tok/s both arms, the ratio and its interval) in rig-log; no decode claim
decide-below: a loss past the margin stops the landing: read the arms' late counts first (a late flip is term 2's
  failure), then the step's lock waits (a profile of the ours arm, perf -e cpu-clock --time) before any re-derivation
decide-above: a gain past the band is a term the derivation missed: read the late counts and fills of both arms
  before it is claimed
